    "q-127055": {
      "dom_id": "step-q127055",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i+(y-x)\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$",
      "worked_solution": "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x+y=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t), \\\\ Q(x,y) & =y-x=3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =9(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =18\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
