    "q-127139": {
      "dom_id": "step-q127139",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$",
      "worked_solution": "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={x}^{2}=49{cos}^{2}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=u,du=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(49{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(1)\\cdot (-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98{cos}^{3}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }-98{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{0}-98{u}^{2}\\,du \\\\  & =(98\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{0}^{2\\pi }+0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
