      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=(1-x)\\,i+(x-y)\\,j$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $16{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=144.$",
      "worked_solution": "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $3$ and $4,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =1-x=1-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =x-y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-12{cos}^{2}⁡t+9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-12{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-12{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+9{\\int }_{0}^{0}u\\,\\text{d}u-12{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }+0-12t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =4(0-0)-12(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =-24\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
