      "source_example_id": "e-9561",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Cartesian Form",
      "kp_id": "e-9561"
    },
    "q-127253": {
      "dom_id": "step-q127253",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14224381/assets/cd21413d8eea5e2fec67eebe22967c846fd7916fd4fb0fbe5f8efe7ac9df6b1a.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y=1-x$ from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,0).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\,i+\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=1-t,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,0).$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14224381/assets/e288ed1095be61e4645fb63c22b7fd2cb605d34795c30557e84afb31b7ec8721.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =1, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1)\\cdot (-1)-(1)\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-2t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-2-0 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
