    "q-127227": {
      "dom_id": "step-q127227",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14224381/assets/85b4da8c0fa35867871b6254ba20f505b7596f2c97226adb2ed01339fe4cbedf.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=5{e}^{-x}\\,i+4\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [1,e],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14224381/assets/559bbc20aa7d47eaad47a8975f80c8efb91bd03bc816357bc6047675dc755c9b.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,y=t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =5{e}^{-x}=5{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}=\\frac{5}{t}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)=\\frac{1}{t}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{5}{t})\\cdot (1)-(4)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t{|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
