      "source_example_id": "e-9574",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Non-Closed Curve",
      "kp_id": "e-9574"
    },
    "q-126940": {
      "dom_id": "step-q126940",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14224381/assets/c71395cfe5877546509f24656ba23f0883cbd3ee8c145c10abae9600c8bd5c4a.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14224381/assets/e90c36e34c6ab51f15707685a22c8bd2c749b9857ca24c59631f09dfbda495c6.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =y={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t)\\cdot (2t)-({t}^{2})\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
